{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792895200,"nonce":"VNaqhIYoCd3Rg582nfyY41iZceE=","title":"SSA: the debug cross-check of the predecessor index scans every block per query","message":"In debug builds `SsaBuilder::current_preds_of` asserts that the incremental `TermIndex` agrees with `scanned_preds_of`, which reads every block's terminator. It runs on every predecessor query, so building a large body is quadratic or worse in a debug build only.\n\nLua conformance hits it: `debug/upvaluejoin_loaded.lua` and `official/calls.lua` time out at the 480 s debug deadline on CI's Linux and macOS runners (seen on aaa974bf and on 39cdefea, run 40149017133). Both load a dumped function with 200 upvalues; `load` compiles it inside `local a1, ..., a200 = _ENV; return function () return a1 + ... + a200 end`, a chain of 200 adds that may dispatch to metamethods, which lowers to thousands of blocks.\n\nOn an M-series Mac (debug zylua, warm-up off): upvaluejoin_loaded.lua 258 s, calls.lua 268 s; the same 100-add chain alone 26 s, with 6353 of 6471 main-thread samples in `scanned_preds_of`. A release zylua runs upvaluejoin_loaded.lua in 0.73 s. Dump-and-load at 25, 50, 100, 200 upvalues: 2.7, 6.4, 39, 197 s.\n\nBounding the check to bodies of at most 512 blocks brings the debug runs to 13 s and 22 s.","files":null}]}